The example map''s S0/S1/U0/U1 are not actual station or depot coordinates. The 600T underground progress is workload; the physical movement, accounting for the 2× underground cost, is 195560mm and 27920mm respectively. Trunk-edge cost validation, control-zone stops, full rejection on blockade, goal replacement, the same function for AI, and hashing reuse the existing time-orders research. The new source-calculation cross-check connects the source derivation, thresholds, load boundaries, squad minimums, and stances with the actual-unit examples. The new evidence path is GAME RESEARCH/verification/campaign-movement-source-20260929/README.md. Do not change the coefficient table to accepted without approvedAt/approvedBy/evidence.

## Current Proposal: Goals, Routes, and World Time

Both players and AI are checked for current position, full route, access rights, layer, and combat lock. A limited research example that can accept a goal replacement has been added. After advancing with existing commands up to the authoritative event, it verifies controllerId, eventTime, and expectedRevision, then atomically accepts a new route that preserves the current edge and the past prefix as they are. Progress W and the remainder are kept. On failure, the existing command and position at the event time are preserved. Mid-edge reversal, changing the current edge''s cost, and full re-search are not implemented.

The unit and personal derivation candidates for 2026-09-29 are specified in MOV-01~07 below. The abstract T/D/W and composite speeds of the existing time-motion/time-orders examples are preserved for verifying the calculation structure. The new source-calculation.mjs cross-checks the personal base values confirmed from the source text and the specified campaign conversions, and is not connected to the product. Using the minimum across all walkers, including the commander, attached heroes, and soldiers, as the squad baseline without excluding non-walkers is a new aggregation proposal.

This is a draft that first seeks approval for the meaning of integer distance u and world-time u, and takes speed as an integer numerator n over a positive denominator d. For an interval''s elapsed time Δt, scaled=n×Δt+remainder, advanced distance=floor(scaled/d), and new remainder=scaled mod d. Intermediate products are computed with BigInt/checked integers, and the remainder is stored so that fractions are not discarded every frame. For the same n, d, and the same route and event interval, applying Δt in split pieces yields the same final distance and remainder. When the goal is reached, progress is clamped to the goal distance, and leftover movement is not saved for the next command.

Elapsed-time intervals are split at authoritative events, and the new speed is not multiplied over the entire past interval. Goal replacement under the conditions of a fixed denominator and a retained current edge is tested with the time-orders example. Denominator changes, stance switches, mid-edge reversal, dynamic blockades, encounters, and exact arrival events are subject to separate approval/verification. The research deals with positions and ledger candidates and does not implement full simultaneous world resolution.

## Current Proposal: Layers, Access, Combat, and Empty World

The surface territory and subway lines are separate layers, and only explicitly designated entrance/transfer edges change layers. Route adjacency, layer, access rights, and blockades are checked, and a command that is illegal from the start is not partially executed. After a valid command is accepted, dynamic blockades/encounters are re-evaluated as time events, and no policy that rewinds distance validly moved in the past is arbitrarily created. Stopping at ZOC endpoints and passage restrictions are review candidates that preserve the existing meaning, but time-based encounter and restart policies must be finalized separately. The old turn costs 750/1000/1250 for road, underground, and transfer are not reused as speed or distance.

Shared online campaign time has no pause, slowdown, or fast-forward usable by players. When the last player leaves, the world stops, and the wall-clock time elapsed while the world was empty is not caught up after reconnecting. A squad in combat receives no new movement commands until the result is settled. The calculator''s battleLocked interval is a minimal proposal that suspends only that squad''s progress while world time continues. Other squads do not stop, and combat does not monopolize world time. The five stance types, inherited/temporary squad leaders, hero/soldier separation, and the soldier maximum of 20 are retained.

## Current Offline Verification and Open Items

GAME RESEARCH/verification/campaign-movement-20260928/time-motion.mjs and time-motion.test.mjs are research examples not connected to the product. Inputting hypothetical A speed 7/3, B speed 5/2, and hypothetical road distance 10 and entrance distance 5, it confirms A distance 11/remainder 2 and B distance 12/remainder 1 at Δt5. The units are integers for testing, not real-world units, and are not approved values. It splits elapsed times 0, 1, 5, 7, and 20 into every possible two-piece division and compares them with whole application, and checks remainder clearing on arrival, empty world, combat lock, illegal route/access rights, integer range, and input immutability.

The source edition, page numbers, and load/HP/FP calculations were cross-checked and recorded in the source table below. The remaining items awaiting approval are MOV-01~07, state transitions, fatigue accumulation/rest, the duration of the forced-march hit penalty, full-map search, dynamic blockades/collisions/return from combat, exact arrival events, denominator changes, ledger order, and retransmission. The new research calculator''s input checks are not an approved-data validator or authenticity verification of approval records. This is not judged as #296 final PASS, #297 ready to start, or Core implementation complete.

Time-based cost candidate: edgeW=ceil(distanceD×workNumerator/workDenominator)+entryWorkW. It requires positive distance/numerator/denominator, non-negative additional work, and a total within the safe integer range. Roads/surface are surface, underground is subway, and only an explicit transfer permits a layer change. Cost errors, blockades, no access rights, and intermediate control zones reject the entire route. Failing to arrive within the horizon is legal progress and not a cost failure. Entry workload is a new candidate whose time varies with speed, and is distinguished from a fixed waiting time.

Synthetic example (not product coefficients): A normal/forced march/ambush = 4/2, 6/2, 2/2 W/T; B = 3/2, 5/2, 1/2 W/T; both garrison/camp = 0/2. All five speeds are input. Forced-march attack/interception cannot be initiated but can be hit, and camp attack cannot be initiated; these are checked separately. Stance changes, concealment detection, and recovery are not implemented. On the map, S0→S1 road 8D×1/2=4W, S1→U0 entrance 2D×1+2W=4W, U0→U1 underground 6D×2=12W, for a total of 20W.

Hand calculation ΔT5: A normal 10W/r0, 2W after U0; B normal 7W/r1 (7.5W), 3.5W during entry. A forced march 15W/r0, B forced march 12W/r1. A normal reaches U1 at 10T; B normal at 13T has 19.5W, and is observed arriving at U1 at 14T (continuous arrival time 40/3T). If B replaces its route at t5 with U0→U2 at a cost of 4W, it keeps the 7.5W, and after +3T×1.5=4.5W arrives at U2 at t8. W is not a physical coordinate, and the 3D interpolation contract is not implemented.

The reachable-range example lists the nodes newly reached within horizonT along the specified route. At ΔT5, A reaches S1 and U0, and B reaches S1. A full-map shortest-path search does not exist yet. If U0 is a control zone, the unit stops at 8W and rejects a general MOVE restart. A blockade on the last edge, no access rights, no actor authority, cost denominator 0/negative/overflow, and a stale revision do not change the existing command, time, or progress. The cost ceil example 8D×1/3=3W is also tested.

The additional research files time-orders.mjs/time-orders.test.mjs use only Node built-in modules. AI and players use the same function, authority, speed, and cost. The command ID, authoritative time, revision, actor, and the semantic fields of cost, route, stance, and progress are serialized in sorted order and chained to the previous SHA256. Display metadata/hostOrder are excluded and route order is preserved. Replaying the same ledger gives the same hash, and a cost change gives a different hash. Time splitting guarantees only the same position; it does not mean that different event ledgers produce the same hash.

The verification packet is in GAME RESEARCH/verification/campaign-movement-20260928, and time-orders-proposal.md contains the derivation, sources, hand calculations, and an open-items table. It passed the existing 24 tests: history 12 + time intervals 4 + events 8. It was also confirmed that nested display metadata does not change the replay hash or route judgment for stops and goal replacement, while actual cost changes are detected. The 12 history tests are not proof of the latest time design. This is an unapproved research fixture, not an approved-data validator, Unity Core, or completed network replay.

## Inputs and events to verify at the 2026-10-01 implementation handoff

Do not rebuild the MOV-01~07 table, and do not treat the fact of a merge as approval. Each row used for implementation must link the approved value, unit, rule version, approvedBy, approvedAt, and evidence in the same input revision. Proposed values, missing approval evidence, approval for a different version, and references that do not match the actual squad or route are all subject to handoff hold. This section states the requirements for an approved-input checker; it does not implement an executable checker or authenticity verification of approval records.

| Acceptance Cases | Input / Action | Expected Result and Responsible Boundary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Screen Switching and World Progress | Switch between the life, strategy, and combat screens while two different squads are moving. | Per #36, hiding a screen must not stop the world time or progress of unrelated squads. Compare the #297 Core record against the #284 display. |
| Domain Without a Station | Use a station-less domain permitted by #37 as the destination, but supply the actual access route as a separate input. | Domain ownership alone must not create a station ID, a subway edge, or free entry. If there is no approved route, do not display it as reachable. |
| Workload and Display Coordinates | Compare progress along edges that have the same distance but different terrain costs. | Do not use W directly as a 3D coordinate or an actual travel distance. Check the projection from workload progress to physical position against the coordinate contract agreed with #279/#284. |
| Arrival, Stance, and Blockade Events | Present inputs in which the arrival time overlaps with a stance change or a dynamic blockade, as well as a change to the speed denominator. | First fix the exact arrival moment, the handling of remainders, the event order, and the restart policy. Where no policy exists, do not treat the result of an existing research example as the product''s correct answer. |
| Distinguishing Combat Locks | While one squad is in combat, issue a movement order to another squad, and observe the end of the engagement and the campaign settlement as separate events. | Campaign movement is locked until that squad''s result is settled. Verify the locking and unlocking of battlefield orders separately under the combat contract. A single shared lock must not replace the two scopes. |
| Missing Approved Input | One of the MOV approval evidence, the #298 formation, the #299 location, or the rule version is missing or inconsistent. | Do not promote it to a product setup or movement order, and do not change the campaign or ledger. Do not substitute the hypothetical A/B and S0/S1/U0/U1 for actual Sinjongmok squads or warehouses. |

The cases above are acceptance specifications to be run after the new Core/API is provided, and they are not pass records. Distinguish them from the existing source-calculation and time-orders verification. The remaining decisions are MOV-01~07, the mapping between world time and wall-clock time, fatigue, rest, and stance events, the order of arrival, collision, and dynamic blockade, and the actual formation, battlefield, and warehouse locations. Add only approved decisions to the corresponding rows, and preserve the historical source.b* and source.sha256.

